**user**:
> Is there a way to use one of my domain names, but have it display content from another domain name? My wife has a site `https://matchedbyjo.my.canva.site/matched-by-jo`, provided for free by Canva, but she doesn't want people to see that URL. She's gotten the url matchedbyjo.com, and wants to display the content there. But downloading the webpage and just uploading it to the server didn't work with all the images (probably too much javascript loading).
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can achieve this by setting up a URL redirection or a reverse proxy. Here's a guide for both approaches:
> 
> ### 1. URL Redirection (Simple)
> 
> This is the easiest method to implement, but the original URL will still be visible in the address bar after redirection.
> 
> - **Step 1:** Set up your DNS settings.
>   - Create a CNAME record for `matchedbyjo.com` pointing to `matchedbyjo.my.canva.site`.
> 
> - **Step 2:** Use an HTTP redirect (not recommended for your purpose, but share for completeness).
> 
> - A simple HTML redirect can be done by creating an `index.html` file for `matchedbyjo.com` with the following content:
> 
>   ```html
>   <!DOCTYPE html>
>   <html lang="en">
>   <head>
>       <meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url='https://matchedbyjo.my.canva.site/matched-by-jo'" />
>       <title>Redirecting...</title>
>   </head>
>   <body>
>       <p>If you are not redirected automatically, follow this <a href="https://matchedbyjo.my.canva.site/matched-by-jo">link</a>.</p>
>   </body>
>   </html>
>   ```
> 
> ### 2. Reverse Proxy (Preferred)
> 
> A reverse proxy method will let you keep `matchedbyjo.com` visible in the address bar while delivering the content from `https://matchedbyjo.my.canva.site/matched-by-jo`.
> 
> - **Step 1:** Set up your DNS settings.
>   - Point `matchedbyjo.com` to the IP address of your server where you'll arrange a reverse proxy.
> 
> - **Step 2:** Configure a reverse proxy on your web server. Instructions below assume you're using Apache or Nginx.
> 
>   #### For Apache:
>   - Enable necessary modules:
>     ```bash
>     sudo a2enmod proxy
>     sudo a2enmod proxy_http
>     sudo systemctl restart apache2
>     ```
> 
>   - Add the following configuration to your site's configuration file:
> 
>     ```apache
>     <VirtualHost *:80>
>         ServerName matchedbyjo.com
> 
>         ProxyPreserveHost On
>         ProxyPass / https://matchedbyjo.my.canva.site/matched-by-jo/
>         ProxyPassReverse / https://matchedbyjo.my.canva.site/matched-by-jo/
> 
>         # Optional: SSL configurations can go here
>     </VirtualHost>
>     ```
> 
>   #### For Nginx:
>   - Add this configuration to your site's configuration file:
> 
>     ```nginx
>     server {
>         listen 80;
>         server_name matchedbyjo.com;
> 
>         location / {
>             proxy_pass https://matchedbyjo.my.canva.site/matched-by-jo;
>             proxy_set_header Host $host;
>             proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
>             proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
>             proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
>         }
> 
>         # Optional: SSL configurations can go here
>     }
>     ```
> 
> After setting up either method, make sure your DNS has been updated accordingly, and test the domain to ensure it behaves as expected. If you need help setting up SSL to secure the site, let me know!
